message: "If you use this thesis, its data or its code, please cite it as below."
title: "MRI of a Mad Machine: The Structural Origins of Hallucination"
abstract: >-
  Which part of a language model turns a defect into a confident error? Three controlled
  studies on 21 language models, with blind double scoring, hypotheses fixed before scoring
  and open data. Study A, the Attention Ablation Test, shows that context mixing decides
  whether a false input becomes an error (attention vs state space at matched data and scale:
  650 vs 566 of 666, p = 2.5e-20). Study B shows that decoding commitment decides how far an
  error spreads (repairing the first wrong fact: 92.5% to 48.1%, p = 5.5e-12). Study C shows
  that the output surface hides the guess (75.3% of wrong answers unhedged; AUROC 0.56). One
  script recomputes 132 reported numbers from the raw files; all match.
